/tmp/tmp.XLsx4uHYp9/src/main.tex:110:At PKC 2009, May and Ritzenhofen \cite{DBLP:conf/pkc/MayR09} introduced the Implicit Factorization Problem (IFP). It concerns the question of factoring two $n$-bit RSA moduli $N _1=p_1q_1$ and $N_2=p_2q_2$, given the implicit information that $p_1$ and $p_2$ share $\gamma n$ of their consecutive least significant bits, while $q_1$ and $q_2$ are $\alpha n$-bit. Using a two-dimensional lattice, May and Ritzenhofen obtained a heuristic result that this implicit information is sufficient to factor $N_1$ and $N_2$ with a lattice-based algorithm, provided that $\gamma n  > 2\alpha n +2$.
/tmp/tmp.XLsx4uHYp9/src/main.tex:112:In a follow-up work at PKC 2010, Faug{\`{e}}re \textit{et al.} \cite{DBLP:conf/pkc/FaugereMR10} generalized the Implicit Factorization Problem to the case where the most significant bits (MSBs) or the middle bits of $p_1$ and $p_2$ are shared. Specifically, they established the bound of $\gamma n > 2\alpha n + 2$ for the case where the MSBs are shared, using a two-dimensional lattice. For the case where the middle bits of $p_1$ and $p_2$ are shared, Faug{\`{e}}re \textit{et al.} obtained a heuristic result that $q_1$ and $q_2$ could be found from a three-dimensional lattice if $\gamma n > 4\alpha n + 6$.
