README.md:16:This generator is based on Feng, Nitaj, and Pan, [*Generalized Implicit Factorization Problem*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.08718), especially Definition 3 and Theorem 3.  A solver receives two exact, equally long, unbalanced RSA moduli.  The two large prime factors are promised to contain the same long consecutive binary block at different unknown positions.  The solver returns the smaller prime divisor of each modulus in displayed order.  Verification is just exact bit-length checking and division; it neither consults the planted answer nor runs a search.
README.md:30:The local executable reference is a bounded-prime scan.  Across eight shipping instances it solved 8/8 after 851,984 trial divisions (106,498 per instance on average) in 0.040653 seconds.  This is easy with code and unrealistic by hand.  The compact route notices
