 ## Caveats
 
-- This is deliberately Track B and a structured subset of GIFP, not a representative distribution of ordinary RSA keys.  Code that tests the reciprocal safe-prime construction solves it immediately.
-- The finite construction gives one small factor 22 bits and the other 23 bits.  Both lie below the same `alpha*n=23` upper bound used in the theorem's small-root estimates, but the factors do not have identical finite bit lengths; the one-bit discrepancy cancels the large-factor shift.
-- The 0/200,000 rate samples uniformly from pairs of primes with the displayed bit lengths.  It does not model a solver conditioned on safe-prime relations or the reciprocal-shift identity.
-- SageMath was unavailable locally, so the paper's LLL/Gröbner implementation was not rerun.  The README reports the paper's Table 3 timing and separately reports the locally executable exhaustive baseline.  ECM, quadratic sieve, number-field sieve, and an external Coppersmith implementation were not tested.
-- Pocklington generation is exact but can become slower at much larger escalated `n`; this affects instance-build time, not verification.
-- Most importantly, OpenRouter quota prevented every bare and G9 oracle call.  Local G–V evidence is complete, but this directory must not be described as oracle-hardened until `harden.py` completes with a real `hardened` verdict and the G9 arms are rerun with scored attempts.
+- This is a deliberately structured GIFP subdistribution, not ordinary RSA-key generation. Code that tests the affine construction solves it immediately; that is why it is Track B.
+- `0/200,000` samples uniformly from ordered pairs of distinct primes with the stated exact bit length. It does not model a solver conditioned on the hidden affine relation, and it is an observed density, not proof that no other valid pair exists.
